, {"key": "ref2", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/978-3-030-60990-0_12"}, {"key": "ref3", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1609/aaai.v37i9.26241"}, {"key": "ref4", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/s13042-020-01167-7"}, {"key": "ref5", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/ICARCV.2006.345353"}, {"issue": "178", "key": "ref6", "first-page": "1", "article-title": "Monotonic value function factorisation for deep multiagent reinforcement learning", "volume": "21", "author": "Rashid", "year": "2020", "journal-title": "Journal of Machine Learning Research"}, {"key": "ref7", "article-title": "Qplex: Duplex dueling multi-agent q-learning", "author": "Wang", "year": "2020", "journal-title": "arXiv preprint arXiv:2008.01062"}, {"key": "ref8", "first-page": "26437", "article-title": "Coordinated proximal policy optimization", "volume": "34", "author": "Wu", "year": "2021", "journal-title": "Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems"}, {"key": "ref9", "article-title": "Maven: Multi-agent variational exploration", "volume": "32", "author": "Mahajan", "year": "2019", "journal-title": "Advances in neural information processing systems"}, {"key": "ref10", "first-page": "3991", "article-title": "Celebrating diversity in shared multi-agent reinforcement learning", "volume": "34", "author": "Li", "year": "2021", "journal-title": "Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems"}, {"key": "ref11", "first-page": "58", "article-title": "Expode: Exploiting policy discrepancy for efficient exploration in multi-agent reinforcement learning", "volume-title": "Proceedings of the 2023 International Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems", "author": "Zhang"}, {"key": "ref12", "article-title": "Influence-based multi-agent exploration", "author": "Wang", "year": "2019", "journal-title": "arXiv preprint arXiv:1910.05512"}, {"key": "ref13", "first-page": "20064", "article-title": "Causality-driven hierarchical structure discovery for reinforcement learning", "volume": "35", "author": "Hu", "year": "2022", "journal-title": "Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems"}, {"key": "ref14", "first-page": "22905", "article-title": "Causal influence detection for improving efficiency in reinforcement learning", "volume": "34", "author": "Seitzer", "year": "2021", "journal-title": "Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems"}], "event": {"name": "2024 IEEE International Conference on Agents (ICA)", "start": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 4]]}, "location": "Wollongong, Australia", "end": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 6]]}}, "container-title": ["2024 IEEE International Conference on Agents (ICA)"], "link": [{"URL": "http://xplorestaging.ieee.org/ielx8/10807380/10807410/10807451.pdf?arnumber=10807451", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 25]], "date-time": "2024-12-25T06:53:01Z", "timestamp": 1735109581000}, "score": 28.128365, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10807451/"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 4]]}, "references-count": 14, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1109/ica63002.2024.00020", "published": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 4]]}}, {"institution": [{"name": "University of Antwerp", "place": ["Antwerp"]}], "indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 12, 16]], "date-time": "2025-12-16T15:18:03Z", "timestamp": 1765898283018, "version": "3.48.0"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "University of Antwerp", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>The significant computational and memory requirements of deep neural networks present a primary obstacle to their deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. The evolution from simple perceptrons to deep neural networks (DNNs), and more recently, large language models (LLMs), has led to an exponential increase in model complexity and, consequently, their computational and memory demands. This trend, coupled with the growing need to deploy models closer to the data source on edge devices with limited resources, makes it challenging to use these powerful models in practice. To address this, the field of neural network compression has gained considerable attention, with pruning emerging as one of the most promising techniques. This thesis focuses specifically on structured pruning approaches, which remove entire structural components such as channels or layers, creating physically smaller networks that can be efficiently processed using standard dense matrix operations. Unlike unstructured pruning methods that create irregular sparsity patterns requiring specialized hardware support, structured pruning enables immediate realization of computational savings on existing (edge) computing platforms without requiring specialized implementations. However, conventional (structured) pruning techniques often rely on simplistic, handcrafted heuristics that inadequately assess the semantic importance of network components and fail to adapt to specific deployment contexts. This can lead to a suboptimal balance between model size, performance, and robustness. To address the limitations of conventional structured pruning methods, this thesis first introduces a novel, knowledge-based pruning methodology that integrates explainable AI (XAI) techniques, specifically Layer-wise Relevance Propagation (LRP), to formulate more informed pruning criteria. This approach facilitates network specialization, a paradigm wherein a pre-trained model is optimized for a specific subtask by systematically removing components that are semantically irrelevant to the target domain. This method achieves higher compression ratios and superior task-specific performance compared to traditional approaches. The limitations of manual pruning schedules are subsequently addressed through an advanced AutoML framework that utilizes reinforcement learning to automate the discovery of optimal pruning policies. The framework incorporates a self-competition reward system to effectively manage the trade-off between compression and accuracy under explicit resource constraints. Furthermore, the decision-making process is enhanced by a global, graph-based network representation, which allows the reinforcement learning agent to consider the entire network topology. This holistic perspective enables the agent to learn pruning strategies directly from data, removing the dependency on predefined, local heuristics. Finally, this thesis investigates the often-overlooked impact of pruning on model robustness. A comprehensive evaluation of compressed models is conducted under various noise conditions using both Monte Carlo simulations and standardized corruption benchmarks. The findings reveal that while structured pruning can negatively affect noise resilience, specialized models pruned with our knowledge-guided techniques can maintain or even enhance robustness for their target domain. Collectively, the contributions presented establish a framework for producing computationally efficient, fast, and robust neural networks tailored for reliable deployment in real-world edge computing environments.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.63028/10067/2192810151162165141", "type": "dissertation", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 12, 16]], "date-time": "2025-12-16T14:49:01Z", "timestamp": 1765896541000}, "approved": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Knowledge-guided adaptive neural network pruning using reinforcement learning and explainable AI"], "prefix": "10.63028", "author": [{"sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7812-0211", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Dieter", "family": "Balemans", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "29262"